{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"a0e8fbc20a9a81ba","name":"card_event_foiled_plans","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/CovertOp/events/card_event_foiled_plans","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FOILED PLANS","text":"When the enemy gives an order, deal damage to the enemy HQ equal to its cost.","flavorText":"The Battle of Kursk in July 1943 marked a turning point on the Eastern Front. Germany had hopes for a decisive victory in Operation Citadel, an offensive proposed by Generalfeldmarschall Manstein, which aimed at encircling and destroying Soviet forces in Kursk. However, Soviet intelligence, aided by Britain, had intercepted German communications, decrypted messages and uncovered the German intentions months in advance. The Red Army prepared extensive defenses which included minefields, fortifications, artillery fire zones and anti-tank strong pointsthat covered approximately 300 km. When the offensive began, the Germans faced a formidable obstacle. The supposed element of surprise was quickly lost, and the battle turned into a brutal war of attrition. Though the Germans made some gains, they were unable to achieve a breakthrough. The Soviets eventually launched a counteroffensive, pushing the Germans back and inflicting heavy losses.","faction":"Germany","type":"gotcha","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_foiled_plans","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_foiled_plans_title","SourceString":"FOILED PLANS","LocalizedString":"FOILED PLANS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::gotcha","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_foiled_plans_text","SourceString":"When the enemy gives an order, deal damage to the enemy HQ equal to its cost.","LocalizedString":"When the enemy gives an order, deal damage to the enemy HQ equal to its cost."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_foiled_plans_history","SourceString":"The Battle of Kursk in July 1943 marked a turning point on the Eastern Front. Germany had hopes for a decisive victory in Operation Citadel, an offensive proposed by Generalfeldmarschall Manstein, which aimed at encircling and destroying Soviet forces in Kursk. However, Soviet intelligence, aided by Britain, had intercepted German communications, decrypted messages and uncovered the German intentions months in advance. The Red Army prepared extensive defenses which included minefields, fortifications, artillery fire zones and anti-tank strong pointsthat covered approximately 300 km. When the offensive began, the Germans faced a formidable obstacle. The supposed element of surprise was quickly lost, and the battle turned into a brutal war of attrition. Though the Germans made some gains, they were unable to achieve a breakthrough. The Soviets eventually launched a counteroffensive, pushing the Germans back and inflicting heavy losses.","LocalizedString":"The Battle of Kursk in July 1943 marked a turning point on the Eastern Front. Germany had hopes for a decisive victory in Operation Citadel, an offensive proposed by Generalfeldmarschall Manstein, which aimed at encircling and destroying Soviet forces in Kursk. However, Soviet intelligence, aided by Britain, had intercepted German communications, decrypted messages and uncovered the German intentions months in advance. The Red Army prepared extensive defenses which included minefields, fortifications, artillery fire zones and anti-tank strong pointsthat covered approximately 300 km. When the offensive began, the Germans faced a formidable obstacle. The supposed element of surprise was quickly lost, and the battle turned into a brutal war of attrition. Though the Germans made some gains, they were unable to achieve a breakthrough. The Soviets eventually launched a counteroffensive, pushing the Germans back and inflicting heavy losses."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":